    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation for `POST /api/owners` driven by the `Idempotency-Key` header.\n\n**What I added:**\n\n1. **`OwnerCreationIdempotencyStore`** (new component in `rest/`) \u2014 a small single-responsibility bean that remembers which owner id a given idempotency key first created (`ConcurrentHashMap`, first-key-wins via `putIfAbsent`). It stores only the id and lets callers re-read the owner, so replays reflect current state.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 wired the store plus an injected `HttpServletRequest` (Spring's thread-bound request proxy) so the controller can read the header the generated `OwnersApi` interface doesn't expose. In `addOwner`:\n   - Two focused private helpers keep the method from becoming a catch-all: `idempotencyKey()` reads/trims the header (blank \u2192 absent), and `replayIfDuplicateKey(...)` returns the original owner with **200 OK** when the key was already seen.\n   - On a genuine create, after `saveOwner` it records `key \u2192 owner.getId()`, so the next repeat replays instead of creating a duplicate (which would otherwise 409).\n\n**Design notes:** the persistence, mapping, and creation logic are reused as-is; the new concern lives in its own unit. Requests without the header behave exactly as before.\n\n**Tests:** the full suite is green \u2014 `Tests run: 4, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` (repeat with the same key returns 200 with the same id).",
